1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:07,512
Hello and welcome to the Owl's Lessons! Last time, you learned how to use the doubling cube.

2
00:00:07,212 --> 00:00:11,955
Today, we're tackling the end of the game: the race and bearing off.

3
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:16,790
This is where a lot of games are decided.

4
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:22,049
And yet, with a few simple rules, you can avoid almost every mistake. Let's go!

5
00:00:25,255 --> 00:00:29,165
At the start of the game, both players' checkers cross paths.

6
00:00:28,865 --> 00:00:32,598
You can block each other, hit each other, trap each other.

7
00:00:33,214 --> 00:00:38,870
But at some point, each player's last checker moves past the opponent's last checker.

8
00:00:38,570 --> 00:00:40,824
Nobody can hit anybody anymore.

9
00:00:41,991 --> 00:00:44,405
From that moment on, it's a race.

10
00:00:44,105 --> 00:00:49,915
There's nothing left to block: the first player to bear off all fifteen checkers wins.

11
00:00:51,949 --> 00:00:54,991
To know who's leading the race, you count pips.

12
00:00:54,691 --> 00:00:59,832
The pip count is the total distance your checkers still have to travel to bear off.

13
00:01:02,148 --> 00:01:09,682
The calculation is simple: for each point, multiply the number of checkers by the point number, then add everything up.

14
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:14,870
For example, three checkers on your eight-point make twenty-four pips.

15
00:01:14,570 --> 00:01:17,634
Two checkers on your six-point make twelve.

16
00:01:18,363 --> 00:01:23,238
At the start of the game, each player has one hundred and sixty-seven pips.

17
00:01:22,938 --> 00:01:25,495
Whoever has the lower count is ahead.

18
00:01:28,009 --> 00:01:32,949
Why does it matter so much? Because the pip count tells you which plan to follow.

19
00:01:33,099 --> 00:01:38,663
If you're leading the race, try to free your checkers and come home without taking risks.

20
00:01:38,363 --> 00:01:40,615
Contact is no use to you anymore.

21
00:01:41,499 --> 00:01:45,942
If you're behind, on the other hand, keep contact for as long as possible.

22
00:01:45,642 --> 00:01:49,246
Your only chance is to hit one of your opponent's checkers.

23
00:01:50,612 --> 00:01:56,543
A useful benchmark: one roll of the dice is worth a little over eight pips on average.

24
00:01:56,243 --> 00:01:59,424
So a ten-pip lead is roughly one roll ahead.

25
00:02:02,719 --> 00:02:08,916
Once you're in a race, you need to bring your checkers home, into your home board, as quickly as possible.

26
00:02:09,936 --> 00:02:12,475
The owl's tip: think in crossovers.

27
00:02:12,175 --> 00:02:18,296
Every time a checker moves from one quarter of the board into the next, that's a crossover.

28
00:02:18,579 --> 00:02:22,216
What matters is getting every checker into your home board.

29
00:02:21,916 --> 00:02:27,984
So move your farthest checkers first, and don't waste time pushing the ones that have already arrived.

30
00:02:28,945 --> 00:02:36,375
With six-five, for example, bring two checkers in from the outer board, rather than pushing a single one all the way to the back.

31
00:02:38,275 --> 00:02:41,718
Second rule of the race: don't waste your pips.

32
00:02:42,607 --> 00:02:46,038
Many beginners pile all their checkers on the six-point.

33
00:02:45,738 --> 00:02:50,342
They get home quickly, but then it takes a lot of rolls to bear them all off.

34
00:02:52,110 --> 00:02:56,497
It's better to spread your checkers over the six, five and four points.

35
00:02:56,197 --> 00:03:00,065
They're all home already, and bearing off will be much faster.

36
00:03:00,773 --> 00:03:05,060
On the other hand, don't fill up the one, two and three points too early.

37
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:10,740
A checker that lands there has used pips for nothing: it would have come off anyway with a small number.

38
00:03:11,788 --> 00:03:18,826
And don't panic if you play one move a little badly. In a race, a single mistake rarely costs the game…

39
00:03:18,526 --> 00:03:20,592
but ten little mistakes do!

40
00:03:23,849 --> 00:03:27,343
All fifteen checkers are home: bearing off begins.

41
00:03:27,043 --> 00:03:31,111
If there's no contact left at all, the rule is very simple.

42
00:03:31,623 --> 00:03:37,239
Bear off a checker every time you can. A checker off the board is one less checker to bring home.

43
00:03:38,321 --> 00:03:42,284
If your die doesn't let you bear off, use it to fill an empty point.

44
00:03:41,984 --> 00:03:46,593
The more spread out your checkers are, the more each roll will let you bear off.

45
00:03:48,454 --> 00:03:54,856
And with a double, you play the same number four times: often that's four checkers off in one go.

46
00:03:54,556 --> 00:03:56,806
The perfect moment to catch up!

47
00:03:59,677 --> 00:04:04,985
Everything changes if your opponent still has a checker in your home board, or on the bar.

48
00:04:04,685 --> 00:04:06,488
Then there's still contact.

49
00:04:08,063 --> 00:04:11,217
Your priority is no longer speed, it's safety.

50
00:04:10,917 --> 00:04:14,940
A single blot hit during the bear-off can cost you the game.

51
00:04:16,648 --> 00:04:22,283
Clear your points starting with the farthest one, and keep an even number of checkers on your highest points.

52
00:04:24,530 --> 00:04:30,091
That way, when you bear off, your checkers leave in pairs, and you avoid leaving a checker on its own.

53
00:04:30,866 --> 00:04:34,786
Sometimes the safest move doesn't bear off any checker at all.

54
00:04:34,486 --> 00:04:38,815
That's fine: better to bear off a little more slowly than to get hit.

55
00:04:41,210 --> 00:04:44,593
To finish, two ideas to keep in mind during the race.

56
00:04:45,139 --> 00:04:47,191
The first is the doubling cube.

57
00:04:46,891 --> 00:04:54,706
In a race, you usually double with about an eight percent lead in the pip count, and you take with up to about twelve percent behind.

58
00:04:55,981 --> 00:05:01,088
Example: with one hundred pips against one hundred and eight, you're eight percent ahead.

59
00:05:00,788 --> 00:05:03,357
That's the right moment to offer a double.

60
00:05:04,239 --> 00:05:06,060
The second is the gammon.

61
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:11,230
If your opponent is already bearing off and you're far behind, your priority changes.

62
00:05:12,999 --> 00:05:19,840
Forget about wastage: bring all your checkers home as fast as you can, and bear off at least one checker.

63
00:05:19,540 --> 00:05:22,207
A single loss is better than a gammon!

64
00:05:23,528 --> 00:05:30,694
And on TickGammon, the analysis of your games shows you the pip count at every move, and points out when you could have played the race better.

65
00:05:34,522 --> 00:05:40,724
And there you go, you now know how to count pips, race without wasting, and bear off your checkers safely!

66
00:05:41,636 --> 00:05:48,535
To practise, play for free against other players or against the computer, in your browser, on Android or on Windows.

67
00:05:48,235 --> 00:05:53,257
Next time, I'll teach you about dice probabilities. Subscribe so you don't miss it!

68
00:05:52,957 --> 00:05:54,964
See you soon around the board!

